{"_data": [{"type": "metadata", "contents": [{"content": "", "type": "lesson_name"}, {"content": "What Is an Algorithm?", "type": "unit_name"}, {"content": 3, "type": "position"}, {"content": "stub", "type": "origin"}, {"content": 1, "type": "lesson"}, {"content": "A1.1", "type": "level"}]}, {"type": "achievment_test", "contents": [{"content": "false", "type": "test"}]}, {"type": "target_word_describer", "contents": [{"content": "These are words you hear when talking about computer algorithms.", "type": "text"}]}, {"type": "objectives", "contents": [{"content": "Identify information about algorithms related to software programming in simple spoken and written situations.", "type": "primary"}, {"content": "Understands key information in simple written and spoken texts about topics related to software programming and its history.", "type": "secondary_1"}]}, {"content": "enpe7n1le01ob03re1img01.jpg", "type": "background_image"}, {"type": "learning_points", "contents": [{"content": "Simple Present Interrogative Form", "type": "heading"}, {"content": "You use the <strong>simple present tense</strong> to talk about facts and events that happen in your daily life like routines or habits.  ", "type": "describer"}, {"content": "You use the <span class=\"strong\"> simple present tense</span> in the interrogative form to ask questions about things that happen frequently.</br> </br>  Remember that it's important to use the auxiliary verb <span class=\"strong\">\u201cdo\u201d </span> or <span class=\"strong\">\u201cdoes\u201d</span> before the subject. </br></br>  Let's see the structure:  </br></br> <span class=\"strong\"> Auxiliary verb do/ does</span> + subject + main verb + complement +? </br></br>  Example: </br></br> <span class=\"italic\"> <span class=\"strong\">Does</span> he understand algorithms? </br></br></span>  Note: In the interrogative form, contrary to the affirmative form, the auxiliary verb changes, and the main verb remains in the base form. </br></br>  Example: </br></br> <span class=\"italic\"> He understands algorithms. </br></br>  <strong>Does</strong> he understand algorithms? </br></br></span>  You can also use Wh- questions to get the information in detail. Let's see the following structure: </br></br> <span class=\"strong\">Wh-question</span> + <span class=\"strong\">auxiliary verb do/ does</span> + subject + main verb + complement+ ? </br></br> Example: </br></br> <span class=\"italic\"> <strong>Where do</strong> you study programming?</span>", "type": "text"}, {"content": "So tell me, why do you need a tutoring session?", "type": "example"}, {"content": "Does the algorithm always work like that?", "type": "example"}]}, {"type": "learning_points", "contents": [{"content": "Algorithms Alternative Approaches and Optimization", "type": "heading"}, {"content": "The Algorithms can solve problems on a computer using a specific procedure.", "type": "describer"}, {"content": "It is necessary to have a procedure (called algorithm), with specific steps that lead to an answer for each computer problem. Some procedures can include looking for specific information on a large source of information. When the algorithm looks word by word for the information, it can take a very long time to obtain an answer because hundreds of algorithms appear at the same time. </br></br>  Some alternative approaches help to optimize the algorithms. For example, the algorithm optimization can find a solution in a fast way, so we can say that one of the most important aspects to improve is the time. ", "type": "text"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "unambiguous", "type": "name"}, {"content": "something that is unclear", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(adjective)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: Last class you said that an algorithm is a series of unambiguous instructions, what does it mean? ", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n1le01ob03aud01", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "subjective", "type": "name"}, {"content": "something that can have different interpretations", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(adjective)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: Well, it means that there is no any kind of subjective interpretation of the algorithm.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n1le01ob03aud02", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "search engine ", "type": "name"}, {"content": "a computer program that searches for information on the web worldwide", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(noun)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: It makes a spell check when you finish a text, or it uses a search engine when you need to seek information.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n1le01ob03aud03", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "optimize", "type": "name"}, {"content": "to make something better, make it best", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(verb)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: Not really, there are now different ways to optimize the process.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n1le01ob03aud04", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "task ", "type": "name"}, {"content": "a specific piece of work assigned to a person or computer", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(noun)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: Every time I present a task to the computer, it uses the same algorithm to solve the problem.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n1le01ob03aud05", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "interpretation", "type": "name"}, {"content": "a conception or idea about something", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(noun)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: It always works like this, and there are no other different interpretations of it.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n1le01ob03aud06", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "takeaw